;; It's quite common to keywordize keys using `keyword`…
(update-keys {"foo" 42 "bar" 1337} keyword)
;; => {:foo 42, :bar 1337}

;; Or stringify them using `name`…
(update-keys {:foo 42 :bar 1337} name)
;; => {"foo" 42, "bar" 1337}

;; But remember this doesn't work recursively for nested structures…
(update-keys {"foo" {"qux" 404} "bar" 1337} keyword)
;; => {:foo {"qux" 404}, :bar 1337}

;; You'll want to reach for `clojure.walk` instead for that…
(clojure.walk/keywordize-keys {"foo" {"qux" 404} "bar" 1337})
;; => {:foo {:qux 404}, :bar 1337}

(clojure.walk/stringify-keys {:foo {:qux 404} :bar 1337})
;; => {"foo" {"qux" 404}, "bar" 1337}

vars.doc:
m f => {k (f v) ...}

  Given a map m and a function f of 1-argument, returns a new map where the keys of m
  are mapped to result of applying f to the corresponding values of m.

